 🥳 Happy Friday! Today's newsletter, edited by Alex Fitzpatrick, is 596 words, a 2-min. read. Thanks to Carolyn DiPaolo for copy editing . 

 ⚡️ Situational awareness: In a much-anticipated speech in Jackson Hole, Wyoming, Federal Reserve chairman Kevin Warsh suggested that interest rate hikes are possible if inflation doesn't fall. More from Neil Irwin and Courtenay Brown . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Roman ready for launch 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 A rendering of the Roman Space Telescope. Image: NASA NASA is preparing to launch the Roman Space Telescope — one of the agency's biggest and most promising scientific endeavors in years, Alex Fitzpatrick reports. 

 🔭 Roman is designed to help answer some of science's most tantalizing mysteries, including questions around dark matter and dark energy.

 Researchers also hope to use it to discover a plethora of new exoplanets — worlds outside our own solar system. 🚀 Launch is set for early Sunday from Florida's Kennedy Space Center aboard a SpaceX Falcon Heavy rocket.

 Roman is carrying two scientific devices: the Wide Field Instrument and the Coronagraph Instrument. 📸 The first is a 300-megapixel near-infrared camera with a field of view 100x that of the esteemed Hubble Space Telescope. The result: much faster data collection and discovery-making.

 The second will be used for high-contrast imaging and spectroscopy of nearby exoplanets to better understand them. (Spectroscopy is a way of studying planets and other objects based on their color.) Roman is lifted into a portable clean room called the "Chariot" for transportation ahead of launch. Photo: Sydney Rohde/NASA 🪐 Researchers hope that data from Roman can help answer lingering questions about two of the universe's least-understood phenomena: dark energy and dark matter.

 Scientists have proposed dark energy as the driving force behind the universe's accelerating expansion. Dark matter is thought to be an invisible "glue" binding the universe together. 🛰️ Jeff Kruk, Roman's deputy senior project scientist, tells Axios: "I can't help but think that the biggest result from Roman 10 years from now is something that we just haven't thought of yet."

